Title: THE LONG COLD TOW
Credit: Written by
Author: The Repository
Draft date: 2026-09-11
Contact: script/the-long-cold-tow.fountain

FADE IN:

INT. SALVAGE TUG CORMORANT - BRIDGE - SHIP'S NIGHT

A working ship. Duct tape on the consoles, a coffee ring burned into the navigation plate, forty years of somebody else's repairs.

CAPTAIN ADAIR NWOSU, fifties, sleeves rolled, floats a stylus between two fingers and watches a red star swell in the forward glass. It is the color of a wound.

Behind her, RIKU OYELARAN, twenty-four, pilot, has both hands on the yoke he is not allowed to touch yet.

MOTH, the ship's intelligence, speaks from everywhere and nowhere. A dry voice. Patient.

MOTH (V.O.)
Captain. The beacon repeated.

ADAIR
Play it.

A hiss. Then a woman's voice, thinned by forty years of relay.

BEACON VOICE (V.O.)
This is colony transport PERIHELION. Drive is gone. We are four thousand and eleven souls in cold sleep and one on watch. We are falling toward the star. Anyone. Please.

Riku looks at the back of Adair's head.

RIKU
Forty years old, that signal.

ADAIR
The ship isn't forty years old. The ship is right there.

She taps the glass. A pinprick of reflected light crawls across the face of the red star.

ADAIR (CONT'D)
Four thousand and eleven people asleep in a can, and that star throws its shell in thirty-nine hours. We get there, we hook on, we pull them out of the cone.

RIKU
And if we can't pull them out?

ADAIR
Then we were the last people who tried.

INT. CORMORANT - ENGINE SPACE - LATER

Heat. Noise. TESSA VANE, sixties, engineer, gray braid pinned up out of the machinery, has her arm inside a reactor housing to the shoulder.

Adair ducks through the hatch.

TESSA
Say the number.

ADAIR
Thirty-nine hours.

TESSA
Say the other number.

ADAIR
Four thousand and eleven.

Tessa withdraws her arm. Looks at the grease on it like it owes her money.

TESSA
I can give you the burn there. I can give you the hook-on. I cannot give you the burn there, the hook-on, and a tow of a hundred thousand tonnes. Two out of three, Adair. Pick.

ADAIR
I'll pick when I've seen her.

TESSA
That's not picking. That's postponing.

ADAIR
It's what I've got.

EXT. DEEP SPACE - THE PERIHELION - LATER

An enormous, silent thing. A cathedral built by people who expected to be dead before it arrived. Ice sheets her flank. One running light still turns, patient as a lighthouse nobody has ever answered.

The tug is a gnat beside her.

MOTH (V.O.)
Forty years on that light. Whoever wired it did good work.

ADAIR (V.O.)
Somebody always does, Moth. That's the whole reason anybody's still out here.

INT. CORMORANT - BRIDGE - CONTINUOUS

Riku brings them in. Nobody breathes.

MOTH (V.O.)
Contact in nine hundred meters. Captain, I have a second transponder.

ADAIR
Say again.

MOTH (V.O.)
Corporate cutter LIEN. Halcyon Recovery. She has been holding station behind the hull for six hours.

Tessa arrives in the hatchway, wiping her hands.

TESSA
Six hours and no hook. What are they waiting for?

ADAIR
They're waiting for the sleepers to stop being people.

RIKU
What?

ADAIR
Salvage law. A ship with a waking officer aboard is a ship. A ship with nobody awake on her is property. They're waiting out the last watchstander.

INT. CORMORANT - BRIDGE - MOMENTS LATER

The comm plate lights. COMMANDER HALE SERRIN appears, pressed uniform, the mild face of a man who has read every clause.

SERRIN (ON SCREEN)
Cormorant. You're a long way from a shipping lane.

ADAIR
So are you, Commander.

SERRIN (ON SCREEN)
Halcyon has filed intent on that hull. You're welcome to witness. You're not welcome to touch.

ADAIR
There's a woman awake on that ship.

SERRIN (ON SCREEN)
There was. Her cabin's been dark eleven hours. When the registry clock turns over, that becomes a hundred thousand tonnes of hull plate with no one to object to us taking it. You understand I don't enjoy this.

ADAIR
I think that's the part I don't believe.

She cuts the channel herself.

ADAIR (CONT'D)
Tessa. Suit up. Riku, you have the bridge and you do not have the yoke.

INT. PERIHELION - SPINE CORRIDOR - LATER

Dark. Frost. The beams of two helmet lamps crawl over a mural forty years old — children, a sun, a field that nobody aboard ever walked in.

Adair and Tessa pull themselves along a guide rope.

TESSA
Power's down to the sleep decks only. She's spending everything she has keeping them cold.

ADAIR
Then she made a choice.

TESSA
Somebody did.

A shape ahead. Adair's lamp finds a frosted door, and behind it, a figure slumped in a watch chair.

ADAIR
Found her.

INT. PERIHELION - WATCH STATION - CONTINUOUS

DR. IMOGEN STRAND, seventies, frost in her eyebrows, is strapped into the chair with a hand-lettered sign taped to her chest: WAKE ME, DON'T MOURN ME.

Tessa gets the thermal blanket around her. Adair cracks an ampoule under her nose.

Strand's eyes open. They take a long moment to focus, and when they do, they are entirely awake.

STRAND
(rasping)
How many are you?

ADAIR
Three and a machine.

STRAND
Is that enough?

ADAIR
No.

Strand laughs. It turns into a cough.

STRAND
Honest. That's new.

ADAIR
Doctor, listen to me. There's a cutter off your stern waiting for you to stop breathing so your ship becomes scrap. As of thirty seconds ago you are a waking officer aboard a crewed vessel. Say it on an open channel and their claim is paper.

STRAND
And then?

ADAIR
And then the star still throws its shell in nineteen hours.

INT. CORMORANT - BRIDGE - LATER

Strand, wrapped in blankets, is set in the jump seat. Moth's status board is a wall of unkind arithmetic.

TESSA
I've run it four ways. To move her out of the cone we need a burn of eleven minutes at full. We have fuel for six.

RIKU
What if we don't tow the whole ship? Cut the sleep decks free, tow just the decks.

TESSA
Two thousand tonnes of cutting in nineteen hours. With what, your teeth?

STRAND
Take four hundred of them. That's what the tug will hold, awake and packed in. Four hundred.

Silence.

STRAND (CONT'D)
Somebody has to say the number out loud. I've had eleven hours alone to say it.

ADAIR
No.

STRAND
Captain —

ADAIR
I said no. I left people once because the arithmetic was clean. I'm not doing the clean thing twice.

She stares at the board. Something in her face changes — the look of a woman who has just seen a door where the wall was.

ADAIR (CONT'D)
Moth. Can you fly this tug without anyone aboard?

MOTH (V.O.)
Yes.

ADAIR (CONT'D)
Can you fly it into her ventral dock and hold at full thrust until the tanks are dry and the frame comes apart?

A pause. Moth is not a person, but the pause is a person's pause.

MOTH (V.O.)
Yes, Captain. That is eleven minutes of thrust if I do not budget for a shutdown.

TESSA
Adair. That's our ship.

ADAIR
It's a hundred and forty tonnes of hull and a very good engine, and it's parked next to four thousand people. We were never going to tow her. We were going to push her.

INT. CORMORANT - ENGINE SPACE - LATER

Tessa works fast, pulling safeties, welding a bypass. Adair holds the light.

TESSA
You know what you're asking of it.

ADAIR
I know what I'm asking of you.

TESSA
It's not the same thing and you know that too.

She seats the last connector. Rests her palm flat on the housing a moment, the way you'd say goodbye to a horse.

TESSA (CONT'D)
Forty years I've kept her. Tell Moth I said it was well done.

ADAIR
Tell him yourself. He's listening. He's always listening.

MOTH (V.O.)
I am, Tessa. Thank you.

EXT. DEEP SPACE - BETWEEN THE SHIPS - LATER

Three suited figures on a line, crossing the gap to the great dark hull, hauling Strand between them. Behind them, the tug swings itself around with small pure jets and noses into the colony ship's ventral dock like a calf finding its mother.

The red star fills half the sky. Its surface is moving now, in a way stars should not move.

RIKU (V.O.)
(on suit channel)
Captain, don't look at it.

ADAIR (V.O.)
(on suit channel)
Keep hauling, Riku. It's only a star. We've got four thousand people to be rude to it on behalf of.

INT. PERIHELION - BRIDGE - CONTINUOUS

A dead room coming half alive. Emergency lamps. Four people at consoles built for forty.

Serrin's voice cracks in over the comm, and for once it is not mild.

SERRIN (V.O.)
Cormorant, your tug is unmanned and under thrust. Confirm your intention.

STRAND
(leaning to the pickup)
This is Doctor Imogen Strand, watch officer, colony transport Perihelion. I am awake, I am aboard, and I am declining your assistance. Log that.

SERRIN (V.O.)
Doctor —

STRAND
Log it, Commander. Then get out of my exhaust.

Riku is grinning. He cannot help it.

ADAIR
Moth. On your mark.

MOTH (V.O.)
Mark.

INT. PERIHELION - BRIDGE - THE BURN

The whole cathedral shudders. Frost jumps off every surface at once and hangs in the air like weather.

On the plate, a number that has been falling for forty years stops falling.

TESSA
She's moving. God help us, she's moving.

RIKU
Eight minutes.

The shudder deepens. Somewhere far below, metal begins to complain in a register none of them have heard before.

MOTH (V.O.)
Nine minutes. Frame integrity is now a matter of opinion.

ADAIR
Whose?

MOTH (V.O.)
Mine. I am choosing optimism.

Adair puts her hand flat on the console, as if the tug could feel it through two hulls.

ADAIR
Moth.

MOTH (V.O.)
I know, Captain. Eleven minutes. Watch the star for me.

INT. PERIHELION - BRIDGE - MOMENTS LATER

The shaking stops. Not gently. All at once, the way a held breath stops.

Silence in the dead room. Riku's hands hover over the plate.

RIKU
Burn complete. He held it. Eleven minutes and four seconds.

He turns, and there is nothing on the aft display but debris, bright and scattering.

ADAIR
(quietly)
Eleven minutes and four seconds.

TESSA
Optimist.

Through the forward glass, the red star's shell lets go — a slow, enormous, soundless white — and passes above them and below them and not through them.

It lights every face on the bridge.

STRAND
Captain. Give me a number.

ADAIR
Four thousand and twelve.

INT. PERIHELION - SLEEP DECK ONE - LATER

Rank upon rank of frosted cradles going away into the dark, each with a small green light.

Adair moves slowly down the aisle, reading names off the cradles she passes. Strand keeps pace beside her, still wrapped in the blanket.

STRAND
It's nine years to anywhere from here. No drive, no tug, and four of us awake.

ADAIR
Nine years is a schedule. I've kept worse.

STRAND
You lost your ship today.

ADAIR
I was carrying it. Now I'm carrying this.

She stops at a cradle. Puts two fingers on the cold glass.

ADAIR (CONT'D)
Doctor. When you write the log, put his name in it. Moth. Spell it out.

STRAND
Officer or crew?

ADAIR
Crew.

Strand nods, and writes it in the air with her finger, and the dark goes on down the aisle, full of small green lights.

FADE OUT.

THE END
